      "chapterTitle": "I. The Tuesday Notification",
      "heading": "I. The Tuesday Notification",
      "body": "The call arrived at fourteen minutes past two on a Tuesday in the middle of the third-quarter regional audit.\n\nSubir Lahiri was sitting at the head of the laminate conference table in Room 304 of the General Assurance Building on Prince Anwar Shah Road, holding an amber plastic highlighter between his thumb and index finger while a junior claims associate from the Siliguri branch explained why four hundred and eighteen flood-indemnity vouchers had been processed without secondary surveyor signatures. The fluorescent tubes overhead hummed with a thin, metallic vibration that always grew louder after lunch, and the air-conditioner in the corner was blowing chilled, recycled air that smelled of damp carpet, damp files, and lukewarm canteen coffee.\n\nSubir's phone sat face-up on the black synthetic leather pad beside his ledger.\n\nIt did not ring aloud; Subir had disabled ringtones in 2014, when he was promoted to regional manager and decided that an audible ring was the mark of a man whose time was not his own. Instead, it gave two long, low pulses against the foam backing of the mousepad—a muted, heavy vibration that rattled the brass clips on the invoice folder.\n\nOn the glass screen, beneath a fine scratch shaped like a crescent moon, the display lit up: Niladri Sen — Mobile.\n\nSubir looked at the screen.\n\nHe did not look with irritation or alarm. He looked at it with the mild, affectionate familiarity one reserves for an old wall clock that runs seven minutes slow. Niladri was in Jamshedpur, working in the structural fabrication division of a heavy-engineering firm near the Telco works, where he had lived for twenty-two years in a company quarter with a red gravel driveway and two drumstick trees behind the kitchen. Niladri called once every six or seven weeks, almost always on a weekday afternoon, almost always when Subir was in the middle of something that could not be paused without an apology to three people sitting across a table.\n\n\"I will take it at four,\" Subir thought, his fingers resting on the smooth plastic cap of the highlighter. \"When the Siliguri claims are signed and the tea tray comes.\"\n\nHe reached out with his left hand, swiped the incoming call icon down into the small red circle, and tapped the pre-set automated message: Can't talk now. Will call you back soon.\n\nThe screen went black. The Siliguri associate continued reading from sheet seven. The fluorescent tube hummed. The Tuesday proceeded along its oiled, bureaucratic groove, and Subir Lahiri forgot the vibration before the associate had finished explaining the transport charges between Jalpaiguri and Malda.\n\nIt was not a betrayal. It was not even a dismissal. It was simply the currency of modern adulthood—the quiet, frictionless habit of borrowing fifteen minutes from a friend who has known you since you were nineteen, under the unspoken assumption that the loan has no interest, no collateral, and no due date.\n\nBetween two and six in the afternoon, thirty-four other things demanded his attention. There was a memorandum from the zonal office in Park Street regarding the revised depreciation schedules for commercial vehicles. There was a frantic phone call from the surveyor in Kharagpur whose motorcycle had broken down near an overturned mustard-oil tanker. There was a clerk named Debabrata whose daughter had been admitted to a nursing home in Garia with suspected dengue, who needed three days of earned leave signed before the payroll system closed at five. Subir signed the leave form, approved the Kharagpur surveyor's local taxi bill, corrected two spelling errors in the Siliguri audit summary, and drank three cups of tea that had sat long enough on the saucer to form a pale, wrinkly skin on the surface.\n\nAt five-forty, his driver, Sanat, brought the car around to the portico. The drive from Prince Anwar Shah Road to the apartment near South City Mall usually took twenty-five minutes through the evening traffic on Jadavpur Central Road. Subir sat in the rear seat of his Honda City, his head resting against the grey velour headrest, his eyes closed against the glare of halogen headlights.\n\nHe felt the phone in his shirt pocket—a warm, solid rectangle resting against his ribs.\n\n\"Call Niladri,\" a small, quiet voice remarked in the back of his mind.\n\nSubir opened his eyes. He unlocked the phone. He opened the call log. Niladri's name sat four entries below the Kharagpur surveyor and two entries above the zonal accountant. The timestamp read: 14:14.\n\n\"No,\" Subir thought, watching the signal turn amber. \"It is six o'clock. In Jamshedpur, Niladri will be driving back from the plant through the Golmuri gate. There will be horn blasts and dust. If I call him now, we will speak for three hurried minutes while he shifts gears. Nilu deserves forty minutes. We need to talk properly about his mother's knee surgery and whether his son passed the engineering entrance. I will reach home, take a hot shower, have a cup of Darjeeling tea on the balcony, and call him at eight.\"\n\nIt was a completely reasonable decision. It was an executive decision, calibrated for quality and attentiveness.\n\nSubir put the phone back into his shirt pocket.",
