      "id": "sec-cp-3",
      "type": "text-image-right",
      "heading": "II. The 07:14 Suburban Local",
      "body": "At eight o'clock that evening, Subir did not call Jamshedpur.\n\nWhen he reached the fourth floor of the flat, his wife Paramita was standing in the kitchen doorway holding a glass container of cumin seeds that had been delivered from an online grocery store with the seal broken. She was angry—not with him, but with the delivery company, with the traffic on the bypass, with the air-conditioner in the guest bedroom that had begun leaking condensate down the newly painted emulsion wall.\n\nThey spent forty-five minutes examining the drip with a flashlight, moving two cartons of books into the corridor, and placing a stainless steel salad bowl beneath the unit to catch the water.\n\nBy the time the bowl was positioned and the tea was brewed, it was twenty minutes past nine. Paramita turned on the television to watch the evening debates, the news anchor's voice shouting through the small apartment like a pneumatic drill in an enclosed courtyard. Subir sat on the dining chair, eating warm chapatis with dry cauliflower curry, staring at his phone where Niladri's missed call was now buried beneath six WhatsApp group messages from his college alumni association debating the renovation of the college canteen.\n\n\"Tomorrow,\" Subir thought, chewing slowly. \"Tomorrow is Wednesday. Wednesdays are quiet. I will call him during the lunch hour from my desk.\"\n\nHe had been saying *tomorrow* to Niladri Sen for approximately eleven years.\n\nIt was not that they had stopped loving each other. In the moral ledger of Subir Lahiri's conscience, Niladri remained his truest friend—the standard by which all other human beings were quietly measured and found lacking in intellectual spine. If someone had asked Subir at a dinner party who he would call if he were arrested on false charges in a foreign country, he would have answered Niladri without blinking. If someone had asked who would care for his daughter if he and Paramita were killed in a highway collision on the Durgapur Expressway, Niladri's name was written into his insurance policy as the primary secondary guardian.\n\nYet in the actual, physical reality of the calendar year 2024, they had spoken exactly three times.\n\nThe first was on January first, a four-minute exchange of generic holiday greetings while Niladri was standing in a vegetable market in Kadma and Subir was pouring orange juice into three glasses at a New Year's breakfast. The second was in May, on Subir's birthday, when Niladri called at seven in the morning and Subir, still half-asleep with a migraine behind his left eye, had promised to call back that evening and then fallen asleep at ten-thirty after taking two paracetamol tablets. The third was in August, when Niladri's mother had undergone knee replacement surgery in Ranchi; Subir had sent a two-thousand-rupee fruit basket via a local florist with a printed card that read *Wishing Aunty a speedy recovery — Subir and Paramita*, and Niladri had texted back: *Aunty smiled at the grapes. Call when you are free.*\n\nSubir had starred the text message. It remained starred in his messaging app, marked with a small golden icon that signaled moral intent without requiring immediate expenditure of energy.\n\nThe truth, which Subir never articulated even to his bathroom mirror, was that talking to Niladri required effort.\n\nNiladri was not a colleague with whom one could exchange twenty seconds of gossip about corporate reorganizations. He was not an acquaintance with whom one could discuss the cricket test match or the municipal elections over salted peanuts. Niladri listened with an intensity that was almost abrasive. When you spoke to Niladri, he remembered what you had told him three years earlier about your fear of becoming your father; he asked whether you had finished reading the translation of Czesław Miłosz you bought in 2002; he wanted to know why you were still staying in the claims department when you had written seventy pages of a monograph on the salt tax under Warren Hastings during your final year of post-graduation.\n\nTo talk to Niladri was to be hauled out of the comfortable, numbing anesthesia of your professional routine and forced to look at the wreckage of the person you had promised to become.\n\nIt was much easier, much safer, to look at the Siliguri flood vouchers.",
