      "id": "sec-cp-6",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "III. The Six Years of Brief Text Messages",
      "heading": "III. The Six Years of Brief Text Messages",
      "body": "On Wednesday, the regional claims manager from Patna arrived unexpectedly with two audit inspectors from the Insurance Regulatory Authority.\n\nThe lunch hour evaporated into three plates of dry vegetable biryani ordered from a hotel across the street, eaten while sitting around the computer terminal reviewing reinsurance treaties from 2019. Subir spent the afternoon defending forty-two claims that had been settled under the emergency flood provisions without notarized affidavits from village panchayats. His tie was loose by three; his throat was hoarse by four; and by five-fifteen, when the inspectors finally packed their briefcases and requested the company car to take them to the airport, Subir had a headache that throbbed in direct rhythm with his carotid artery.\n\nHe sat alone in Cabin 304, the lights half-dimmed, rubbing the bridge of his nose where his spectacles had left two red depressions in the skin.\n\nHe picked up his phone.\n\nNiladri's name was still there. Twenty-four hours had passed since the two vibrations against the mousepad.\n\nSubir opened their chat history. It was like excavating a core sample from an Antarctic ice sheet, where the lower layers were rich with life and the upper layers were thin, brittle, and frozen.\n\nIn 2016, there were long paragraphs. Niladri would send three-page essays about the design of a bridge girder over the Subarnarekha river, quoting passages from Ruskin on the honesty of iron versus the deceit of plaster. Subir would reply at midnight with reflections on an essay by Isaiah Berlin or a memory of their third-year chemistry professor who always smelled of bitter almonds from potassium cyanide titrations.\n\nIn 2018, the paragraphs became sentences.\n\n*Niladri: Subir-da, am in Kolkata for two days for a materials conference at BESU. Free tomorrow evening?*\n*Subir: Nilu, terrible timing! In Bangalore for zonal conference until Friday. Next time you come, let me know five days in advance so I can block the evening.*\n\nIn 2020, during the first lockdown, there had been a brief, frantic resurgence. They had spoken on the phone for two hours while sirens wailed in the empty streets outside their shuttered balconies. Niladri had described the eerie silence of the Telco factory floor, where thirty thousand tons of cold steel lay motionless under the corrugated roof, while Subir described the panic of trying to find oxygen cylinders for his wife's uncle in Salt Lake. For a few weeks, the shared terror of mortality had stripped away the polite bureaucratic armor, and they had been nineteen again, huddled together on the Baker Laboratory stairs.\n\nThen the vaccine arrived; the offices reopened; the loss-ratio spreadsheets resumed their demanding rhythm; and the messages shrank into transactional shorthand.\n\n*2022: Happy Diwali, Subir-da. Hope all is well.*\n*2022: Happy Diwali, Nilu. Hope the family is doing well.*\n\n*2023: Subir-da, heard about your daughter going to Toronto. Congratulations. Big milestone.*\n*2023: Thanks Nilu. Very hectic with visa and forex. Will call this weekend.*\n\n*2024: Subir-da, no disaster. Just standing by the Kharkai river. Pick up when you breathe.*\n\nSubir read that last message three times.\n\nIt had been sent on October 14th, at six-fifteen in the evening. Subir remembered that evening: he had been sitting in the departure lounge of Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose Airport, waiting for a delayed flight to Guwahati, his lap covered with printouts of the annual performance appraisal forms for sixty-two regional field officers. He had seen the message pop up; he had felt that familiar, comforting warmth in his chest; and he had thought: *When I return from Guwahati on Sunday, I will have a free morning. Sunday morning at ten. I will make a pot of tea, sit in the armchair with the phone, and we will talk for an hour.*\n\nSunday morning had arrived. His sister-in-law had come over with her son to discuss college admissions in Pune; the water purifier had leaked over the kitchen counter; and then Sunday was gone, Monday was there, and the Guwahati file was replaced by the Siliguri audit.\n\nFourteen days had passed since that text message.\n\nSubir looked at the clock on his phone display: 17:42.\n\n\"I will call him now,\" he said aloud to the empty office.\n\nHe pressed the call button.\n\nThe screen shifted to the outgoing call display: *Calling Niladri Sen...*\n\nIt rang once. A long, clean, mechanical buzz that echoed in the receiver against his ear.\n\nThen his office landline rang—the heavy, shrill electronic two-tone warble of the direct line from the Managing Director's secretariat on the eighth floor.\n\nSubir froze.\n\nThe mobile phone was ringing against his right ear; the desk telephone was screaming at his left elbow. On the desk telephone, the red priority LED was blinking—the signal that meant the Director was on the line personally, not his executive assistant.\n\nSubir looked from the mobile screen to the blinking red light.\n\nHis left hand moved automatically. It was not a conscious decision; it was the trained, autonomic reflex of thirty years in corporate hierarchy. He tapped the red button on the mobile phone, disconnecting the call to Jamshedpur on the second ring, and lifted the desk receiver with his left hand.\n\n\"Yes, Sir,\" Subir said, his voice dropping into that smooth, respectful, impenetrable register he had practiced for two decades. \"Subir here.\"\n\n\"Subir,\" the Managing Director's voice was crisp, dry, entirely focused on numbers. \"The head office has questions on the third-quarter reserve allocation for the tea-garden flood claims in Assam. I need you in the boardroom with the reinsurance split in ten minutes.\"\n\n\"Right away, Sir,\" Subir said. \"I am coming up.\"",
