      "chapterTitle": "III-B. The Letters from the Blast Furnace",
      "heading": "III-B. The Letters from the Blast Furnace",
      "body": "In the first thirty months after graduation, before mobile handsets became common in the industrial townships of Bihar, Niladri had written forty-two letters to Subir on thin, blue inland letter forms.\n\nSubir had kept them in an empty cardboard shoebox that once held a pair of black Bata formal shoes.\n\nThe inland letters were folded into three panels, sealed with a circular gummed flap, and stamped with the circular postmark of the Tatanagar Head Post Office. In those days, Niladri was an apprentice metallurgist in the foundry shop of the locomotive division. His handwriting was dense, sloping slightly to the left, written with a blue Reynolds ballpoint pen that occasionally left oily smudges on the cheap newsprint paper:\n\n*14 November 1999: Subir-da, you cannot imagine the scale of the cast-house. When they tap the furnace at four in the morning, the liquid iron flows down the sand troughs like molten sun. The air smells of burning limestone and ozone. The senior foreman, an old Sikh gentleman named Harbhajan Singh who lost three fingers in a roller mill in 1974, told me today that steel does not care about your degree marks. If you cool it too quickly, it shatters; if you cool it too slowly, it develops coarse pearlite and loses tensile strength. Everything in this life, Subir-da, is a question of cooling rate.*\n\n*28 February 2000: Subir-da, the company hostel has thirty-six rooms and one shared black-and-white television in the recreation hall. On Sunday evenings, forty young men sit on wooden folding chairs watching Chitrahaar while eating dry bread with potato curry. Most of them talk about two things: getting transferred to the export office in Bombay, or getting married to a girl whose father owns land in Burdwan. When I try to talk about Bernal or the early chapters of Das Kapital, they look at me as if I have escaped from an asylum. I miss our tea at the College Street stall. I miss the rain coming through the broken louvers of the library.*\n\n*12 August 2000: Subir-da, congratulations on joining General Assurance. Twelve hundred rupees a month plus medical allowance is very solid for a first appointment. But promise me one thing: do not let them turn you into an accountant of other men's catastrophes. An insurance company is built on the statistical probability of disaster. If you spend forty years calculating the likelihood of fires, sinkings, and train derailments, you will begin to view every human being as a liability waiting to mature.*\n\nSubir had read those letters under the single forty-watt bulb in his rented barsati in Dhakuria.\n\nHe had replied faithfully for the first year. His own letters had been enthusiastic, describing the excitement of his first inspection tours in the tea gardens of Jalpaiguri, the smell of damp jute warehouses in Cossipore, and his pride at buying his mother a Godrej pressure cooker with his third salary cheque.\n\nThen Subir's mother had fallen ill with chronic rheumatoid arthritis; Subir had met Paramita at an inter-collegiate debate symposium at Rabindra Bharati University; the regional manager had promoted him to senior underwriter; and the inland letter forms had been replaced by quick three-minute calls from the corner PCO booth on Saturday evenings.\n\n*Subir: Nilu, got your letter. Very busy with the quarterly reinsurance statement. Will write a long letter next weekend.*\n\nThe long letter had never been written.\n\nBy 2003, Niladri had bought a mobile phone with a monochrome screen and a rubber keypad. The letters had stopped. The shoebox had been taped shut and pushed behind the extra winter quilts on the top shelf of the bedroom almirah, where it had remained untouched for twenty-one years.",
