      "id": "sec-cp-8",
      "type": "image-left-text",
      "heading": "IV. The Blue Pocket Diary",
      "body": "The boardroom meeting lasted until eight-thirty. By the time Subir descended to the lobby, the rain had started—a heavy, cold late-October shower that washed the dust from the gulmohar leaves along Prince Anwar Shah Road and turned the tram tracks into greasy black mirrors.\n\nHe went home. He ate cold rice with lentil soup. He slept with the heavy, dreamless exhaustion of a man who has surrendered his waking hours to other people's ledgers.\n\nThursday came and went with thirty-eight emails about tea-garden reinsurance.\n\nFriday arrived with a union delegation from the drivers' cooperative demanding higher overtime allowances for outstation surveys.\n\nThen Saturday morning arrived.\n\nSaturday was October 28th. The morning was clear, cool, washed clean by the overnight rain. The autumn sun lay across the balcony floorboards in long, pale rectangles that smelled of drying lime plaster. For the first time in three weeks, Subir did not have an audit to attend, an associate to supervise, or a flight to catch. He was wearing his old flannel pyjamas, sitting in the cane chair with a blue ceramic mug of second-flush tea, reading the literary supplement of the morning newspaper.\n\nParamita was in the kitchen, listening to a recording of Kishori Amonkar singing Raga Bhupali on her tablet.\n\nAt six-forty in the morning, Subir's phone vibrated on the glass table.\n\nSubir looked at it over the rim of his reading glasses. The display did not show Niladri Sen. It showed an unfamiliar ten-digit number starting with the prefix 9431—a Jharkhand BSNL mobile series.\n\nSubir picked it up. He almost swiped it away; on Saturday mornings, seventy percent of incoming calls from unfamiliar numbers were automated loan offers or credit-card promotional bots from call centers in Salt Lake. But something in the early hour—six-forty—made him pause. Automated callers did not begin before nine.\n\nHe swiped the green icon to the center.\n\n\"Hello?\" Subir said.\n\nOn the other end of the line, there was a strange, hollow sound—the sound of an empty verandah where wind was rustling through a corrugated plastic sunshade. Then a woman's voice, high, tight, trembling with the desperate, brittle composure of someone who has been rehearsing five sentences for two hours:\n\n\"Is this Subir Lahiri-da?\"\n\nSubir sat up straighter in the cane chair. The ceramic tea mug clicked against the glass tabletop.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"This is Subir. Who is speaking?\"\n\n\"Subir-da... I am Ananya. Niladri's sister.\"\n\nThe name took four seconds to clear the silt of thirty years. Ananya—the skinny twelve-year-old girl with two plaits who used to bring them cups of hot tea when Subir stayed overnight at Niladri's family home in Ranaghat before Niladri's father died. She was married now; she lived in Dhanbad; Subir had sent a silver coin for her daughter's rice ceremony seven years ago.\n\n\"Ananya,\" Subir said, his voice softening into warmth. \"Yes, Ananya, how are you? Is everything—\"\n\n\"Subir-da,\" she interrupted. She did not raise her voice; she spoke with a terrible, flat precision. \"Nilu-da had a cerebral hemorrhage yesterday evening at five o'clock. In the car, coming home from the plant. By the time they reached the Tata Main Hospital, the doctors said there was nothing to do. He passed away at midnight.\"\n\nThe words arrived through the speaker with absolute acoustic clarity.\n\nThey did not sound loud. They sounded like five dry pebbles falling into a deep, empty brass vessel.\n\nIn the kitchen, Kishori Amonkar continued to sing the ascending notes of the raga, the tanpura humming with serene, indifferent perfection. On the balcony floor, the pale rectangle of morning sun did not tremble. The crows on the electric wire outside the parapet continued preening their grey neck feathers in the autumn light.\n\nSubir held the phone against his ear.\n\n\"What did you say?\" he whispered.\n\n\"He was alone in the car,\" Ananya said, her voice finally breaking into dry, gasping sobs. \"He had pulled over to the side of the road near the river bridge. His phone was on the seat next to him. When the police brought his belongings to the hospital, his last outgoing call was to your number on Tuesday afternoon at two-fourteen. The nurse said... the nurse said he had tried to call you again on Friday at five-thirty, but the phone was locked with his thumbprint and we couldn't open it until this morning.\"",
