      "id": "sec-cp-10",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "V. The Morning of October 28th",
      "heading": "V. The Morning of October 28th",
      "body": "The human brain has an astonishing capacity to protect itself from immediate agony by retreating into practical logistics.\n\nSubir did not collapse. He did not drop the mug. He asked Ananya three clear, sensible questions: Where was the body? Had Niladri's wife, Shampa, been informed? When was the cremation scheduled?\n\n\"At the Subarnarekha burning ghat,\" Ananya wept. \"At two o'clock this afternoon. Shampa is in shock. The factory union has arranged the ambulance. Subir-da... can you come?\"\n\n\"I am coming,\" Subir said.\n\nHe hung up. He stood up from the cane chair.\n\nParamita came out of the kitchen with a plate of sliced papaya. She looked at his face and stopped walking three feet from the balcony door.\n\n\"Subir?\" she said. \"What happened? Who was that?\"\n\n\"Niladri is dead,\" Subir said.\n\nHe said the three words with the exact same tone he used when telling her that the municipal water supply had been turned off for maintenance. He walked past her into their bedroom, opened the teak almirah, took out a clean white cotton kurta, a pair of dark trousers, and his small leather overnight bag.\n\n\"Niladri?\" Paramita stared at him, her hands trembling around the edge of the ceramic plate. \"Niladri from Jamshedpur? But he was forty-six! He had no illness! How can he—\"\n\n\"A hemorrhage,\" Subir said, folding the trousers with mechanical neatness. \"Yesterday evening. The cremation is at two. I am taking the car to Howrah station to catch the Ispat Express.\"\n\n\"Subir, let me come with you,\" she said, reaching for his arm.\n\n\"No,\" Subir said softly, not looking at her. \"The Ispat leaves at eight-fifteen. There is only one seat on the waiting list. I will go alone.\"\n\nHe drove to Howrah through the Saturday morning stillness of Kolkata. The city was waking up: sweepers with long bamboo brooms scraping dried leaves along the curb; milkmen balancing four aluminium cans on the crossbars of their black Hercules bicycles; the vast, red-brick façade of Howrah station rising over the brown water of the Hooghly like an imperial fortress.\n\nSubir bought a tatkal ticket at counter seven. The train was the 12871 Ispat Express, departing platform eighteen at eight-twenty. He climbed into the air-conditioned chair car, found his seat by the window—Seat 44—and sat down with his leather bag between his feet.\n\nAs the train shuddered, cleared its pneumatic brakes, and slid out of the dark cavern of the station into the morning sunlight over the marshy flats of Tikipara, Subir opened his phone.\n\nHe did not look at his emails. He did not check whether the Siliguri claims summary had been approved by the zonal committee.\n\nHe opened his call log.\n\nHe scrolled down to Tuesday, October 24th, 14:14. *Niladri Sen — Mobile. Incoming. Disconnected.*\n\nThen he scrolled to Friday, October 27th, 17:42. *Niladri Sen — Mobile. Outgoing. Disconnected.*\n\nThe two records sat fifty lines apart in the digital memory of the device. Between them lay thirty-four business calls, twelve WhatsApp exchanges about reinsurance treaties, and eighty-four hours of an ordinary working week.\n\nAt 17:42 on Friday, Subir had disconnected his call after one ring because the Managing Director's desk line had blinked. At 17:42 on Friday, five hundred miles to the west, Niladri Sen had been sitting in his car beside the river bridge in Jamshedpur, feeling the sudden, blinding explosion of blood in his occipital lobe, reaching out with dying fingers to dial the one number that had been his anchor for thirty years.\n\nAnd Subir had hung up to talk about tea gardens.",
