      "chapterTitle": "V-B. The Journey Across the Red Soil",
      "heading": "V-B. The Journey Across the Red Soil",
      "body": "The Ispat Express cleared the industrial sprawl of Howrah and entered the open paddy fields of Hooghly district.\n\nThrough the double-glazed tinted window of Seat 44, the rural landscape unfolded in long, sweeping horizontal bands: the brilliant, watery emerald of the late autumn aman rice crop; the dark, reflective silver of village ponds fringed with water hyacinths; the whitewashed concrete spires of small village temples flashing past between groves of betel nut palms.\n\nSubir pressed his forehead against the cool acrylic pane.\n\nBeside him, an elderly gentleman in a khadi waistcoat was reading an English financial daily, his spectacles perched on the tip of his nose, his thumb turning the peach-colored pages with a sharp, dry rustle every three minutes. Across the aisle, a young mother was peeling an orange for her four-year-old son, the sharp, pungent scent of citrus oil cutting through the stale, chilled air of the carriage.\n\nEverything around him was functioning with unremarkable, mundane regularity.\n\nA vendor in a blue railway apron came down the aisle with a wire basket of warm vegetable cutlets: \"Cutlet, bread-omlette, hot coffee, tea...\"\n\nSubir did not look up.\n\nHis mind was traveling thirty years in reverse, along the exact same railway track.\n\nIn December 1997, after their fifth-semester examinations, he and Niladri had taken this exact train to go to Chaibasa, where Niladri's uncle was an assistant conservator of forests. They had no reservations; they had bought two unreserved tickets at Howrah for nineteen rupees each and spent four hours sitting on their canvas rucksacks in the crowded vestibule near the toilet, passing a paper cone of roasted spiced peanuts between them.\n\nNiladri had spent the journey reading aloud from a cheap paperbound volume of Jibanananda Das:\n\n*Ami jodi jhor hoi, shondhar jhor...*\n*If I were a storm, an evening storm in the wild grass...*\n\nThe ticket collector had come through the vestibule, a fierce-looking man with a grey mustache and a brass badge that read *S. K. Ganguly, Travelling Ticket Examiner*. He had demanded their tickets with a scowl, ready to fine two scruffy college boys who looked like student union agitators. But Niladri had engaged the inspector in a conversation about the history of the Bengal-Nagpur Railway, quoting the exact mileage between Kharagpur and Sini Junction, explaining the metallurgical advantages of British steel sleepers over local sal wood ties, until the collector had forgotten his scowl, leaned against the handrail, and spent twenty minutes telling them about his eldest son who was studying electrical engineering in Shibpur.\n\nWhen the inspector moved on without fining them, Subir had laughed until his ribs ached.\n\n\"Nilu,\" Subir had said, wiping tears of mirth from his cheeks, \"you could talk a hangman into giving you his rope as a souvenir.\"\n\n\"Language is not a rope, Subir-da,\" Niladri had replied, looking out at the red laterite soil of Jhargram rushing past in the dusk. \"Language is the only bridge we have across the canyon between two skulls. If you stop talking to someone, you are not punishing them; you are simply stepping off the bridge and letting them fall into the dark.\"\n\nThe train blew its long, mournful dual-tone horn as it crossed the bridge over the Kangsabati river near Midnapore.\n\nThe bridge ironwork rattled overhead—a rapid, staccato clatter of steel girders that sounded like machine-gun fire: *clack-clack-clack-clack-clack*.\n\nSubir closed his eyes.\n\nHe had stepped off the bridge. He had not stepped off in anger; he had stepped off because his briefcase was heavy, his promotion was approaching, and his calendar was full of appointments with men whose names he would forget three years after retirement.",
