      "chapterTitle": "VI. The Steel City in the Afternoon",
      "heading": "VI. The Steel City in the Afternoon",
      "body": "The train crossed the Subarnarekha river at one-fifteen in the afternoon.\n\nThe water was low, braided between broad sandbars of yellow silt, reflecting a sky that had turned pale, hazy, bleached by the autumn heat. On the horizon, the dark, rounded domes of the Dalma hills rose above the chimneys of the industrial works like sleeping mammoths.\n\nSubir got down at Tatanagar station.\n\nThe platform smelled of coal smoke, hot lubricating oil, and stale samosas. He did not take a yellow taxi; he walked to the auto-rickshaw stand outside the south gate, gave the driver the address in Telco Colony, and sat in the narrow rear seat with his leather bag on his knees.\n\nThe road wound through the planned geometry of the steel city: avenues of flowering gulmohar and neem trees planted by company horticulturalists in the 1950s; neat brick bungalows behind low boundary walls; the massive concrete gates of the foundry and axle divisions where shifts of workers in blue uniforms were changing under the whistle. Everything was orderly. Everything was engineered for durability, for load-bearing capacity, for structural resilience.\n\nThe auto-rickshaw stopped outside Quarter 14-B on Road 8.\n\nThe house was a single-storey brick bungalow with a sloping tiled roof, painted company-standard pale cream with green trim along the eaves. In the driveway, beneath the shade of the two drumstick trees, forty or fifty people were standing in the quiet, awkward clusters that form around sudden bereavement. Men in white shirts with company security badges pinned to their pockets; neighbors carrying steel jugs of drinking water; two young engineers from the fabrication shop looking at their shoes in silence.\n\nIn the center of the verandah, on two blocks of ice covered with wet gunny sacking, lay Niladri Sen.\n\nHis face was uncovered.\n\nSubir walked up the gravel driveway.\n\nHis leather shoes crunched on the small red stones—a sound so loud, so intrusive, that three engineers turned to look at him. Subir did not see them. He saw only the face on the ice.\n\nNiladri looked smaller than Subir remembered. In Subir's memory, Niladri was six feet tall, broad-shouldered, leaning against the railing of the Baker Laboratory with a cigarette between his fingers and his hair wild in the Nor'wester gale. The man on the ice was grey at the temples; his cheeks had sunken slightly where his dentures had been removed; his hands, crossed over his chest beneath a white cotton sheet, were rough, calloused, the knuckles stained with dark grease that thirty years of industrial soap had never entirely dissolved.\n\nNiladri's wife, Shampa, was sitting on a low wooden stool beside the ice.\n\nHer hair was uncombed; her forehead was bare where the vermilion had been washed away; her eyes were swollen into two dark, bruised slits that looked at Subir without recognition, then with sudden, terrifying comprehension.\n\n\"Subir-bhai,\" she whispered. Her voice was cracked, raw, like dry leaves scraping over concrete. \"He waited for your call on Friday. He told me on Wednesday morning... he said, 'Subir must be in an audit. Subir always calls on Friday when the week ends.'\"\n\nSubir did not speak.\n\nHe could not speak. The muscles of his throat had contracted into a hard, fibrous knot that choked the air from his trachea.\n\nHe dropped to his knees on the cold red oxide floor of the verandah, beside the block of ice, and laid his forehead against the hem of the sheet covering Niladri's feet.",
