      "chapterTitle": "VI-B. The Unfinished Monograph on the Salt Tax",
      "heading": "VI-B. The Unfinished Monograph on the Salt Tax",
      "body": "Before they left for the ghat, Ananya had unlocked the small study room behind the verandah.\n\nIt was a narrow room, ten feet by twelve, with a single high window facing the northern boundary wall. The room smelled intensely of Niladri: a mixture of pipe tobacco, dried ink, engine oil, and old paper. Along the left wall stood three teak bookcases built by a local carpenter from Jugsalai, their shelves bowed slightly under the weight of engineering textbooks, Russian translations of metallurgical treatises, and hundreds of Bengali literary periodicals from the 1970s and 1980s.\n\nOn the middle shelf, between a volume on the continuous casting of billets and a dictionary of technical German, sat a thick cardboard binder covered in marbled green paper.\n\nSubir reached out and touched the spine.\n\nHis fingertips recognized the texture before his eyes could read the faded white label pasted on the cover. Written in his own precise, nineteen-year-old cursive hand was the title:\n\n*A Fiscal and Human Inquiry into the Salt Monopolies of the Bengal Presidency, 1772–1785 — Subir Lahiri & Niladri Sen.*\n\nSeventy-two handwritten pages on ruled foolscap paper.\n\nIn their final year of college, when their classmates were attending coaching institutes for the civil service preliminary examinations or practicing group discussion techniques for bank probationary officer interviews, Subir and Niladri had spent their Saturday afternoons in the National Library in Alipore. They had combed through the colonial records of the Board of Trade, transcribing the letters of British salt agents who had taxed the coastal distillers of Midnapore and the Sundarbans until whole villages had fled into the tidal marshes.\n\nThey had believed, with the ferocious, beautiful arrogance of twenty-year-olds, that they were writing an important historical work.\n\n\"We will publish it with the Asiatic Society,\" Niladri had declared one stormy evening on the steps of the library, while thunder shook the banyan trees along Belvedere Road. \"We will show how bureaucratic systems do not become monstrous by design; they become monstrous because ordinary men sit in offices and sign forms that treat human hunger as an accounting variance.\"\n\nSubir opened the binder.\n\nOn page forty-four, where the text discussed the salt riots of Tamluk in 1781, there was a margin note written in purple ink in Niladri's hand:\n\n*Subir-da: Check the East India Company records for the Salt Agent at Hijli. Did he resign because he had a conscience, or did he resign because his pension was secured? Most men wait until their pension is secured before they remember they have a soul.*\n\nSubir stared at the purple ink.\n\nThe ink was thirty years old, but the words felt as sharp as a newly honed razor blade drawn across his knuckles.\n\nNiladri had kept the binder. Through three job transfers, through the birth of two children, through twenty-two years of living in company quarters in the steel city, Niladri had kept the seventy-two pages of their unfinished youth on the middle shelf of his bookcase.\n\nHe had kept it because he believed that someday, when Subir's audits were completed and the reinsurance splits were balanced, Subir would come down to Jamshedpur on a Friday evening, sit in the cane chair under the drumstick trees, take out an ink pen, and write page seventy-three.\n\nSubir closed the binder. He held it against his chest with both arms, pressing the hard cardboard edges into his collarbones, and felt the first tear break through thirty years of professional restraint, running hot and salt-bitter down the crease of his cheek.",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1544717305-2782549b5136?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=85",
      "alt": "A thick antique green marbled ledger notebook on a wooden study shelf",
      "caption": "The seventy-two pages on the Bengal salt monopoly, preserved on Niladri's shelf for twenty-seven years.",
