      "heading": "VII. The Verandah in Telco Colony",
      "body": "The cremation took place at three-thirty on the banks of the Subarnarekha.\n\nSubir stood in the crowd of workers and family members, breathing the smoke of dry mango wood and burning ghee, watching the flames rise toward the pale afternoon sky. He did not cry. Grief, when it arrives with the sudden weight of a tectonic shift, does not produce tears; it produces a profound, deafening silence inside the chest, as if the interior of the body has been stripped to bare brick.\n\nAfter the rites were completed and the family had returned to the house to drink the bitter neem water required by ritual, Ananya brought out a small cardboard carton from Niladri's study.\n\n\"Subir-da,\" she said, sitting beside him on the cane sofa in the drawing room. \"These were in his desk drawer. Shampa-bhabhi wanted you to have them.\"\n\nSubir opened the box.\n\nInside was a hardcover notebook bound in black buckram—their joint practical chemistry laboratory journal from Presidency College, dated 1998, its cover stained with faint yellowish rings of nitric acid. Inside the front flap was a photograph of the two of them taken at Puri in 1999: two boys in ill-fitting swimming trunks standing knee-deep in the grey surf, their arms slung around each other's shoulders, squinting into the blinding glare of the noon sun, laughing with that reckless, arrogant certainty that the world belonged to them.\n\nTucked beneath the photograph was a folded slip of blue bond paper with four lines written in Niladri's familiar, hurried handwriting:\n\n*Subir,*\n*When you read this, you will probably be a general manager with three secretaries and an ulcer.*\n*Remember: the salt tax didn't kill the empire because of the money. It killed it because nobody had the courage to tell the viceroy that people were starving.*\n*Don't become a viceroy, Subir-da. Call when you are free.*\n\nSubir held the paper between his fingers. The ink was slightly faded, but the paper still smelled of the old Ranaghat house—of dry neem leaves, naphthalene, and rainy afternoons thirty years ago.\n\nHe sat on the verandah until the sun went down behind the Dalma hills.\n\nThe red gravel driveway turned purple, then black. The two drumstick trees rustled in the evening breeze coming off the river. One by one, the factory colleagues shook his hand, murmured condolences, and walked out of the gate, their footsteps fading down the quiet colony road.\n\nSubir took his phone from his pocket.\n\nHe unlocked the screen. The display lit up with eighty-four notifications: twelve urgent emails from the regional office, six text messages from surveyor teams in Malda, a calendar reminder for a compliance meeting on Monday morning at nine-thirty.\n\nHe looked at the notifications.\n\nFor the first time in his adult life, he looked at them and felt nothing. No panic, no urgency, no professional pride. They looked like dry insect husks trapped in a spiderweb—brittle, meaningless, entirely devoid of life.\n\nHe opened his messaging app. He found the voice memo from October 14th.\n\nHe pressed play, held the phone against his ear, and listened to Niladri's voice in the dark:\n\n\"Subir-da, no disaster. Just standing by the Kharkai river watching the fog lift off the weir. Pick up when you breathe.\"\n\nSubir closed his eyes.\n\n\"I am breathing now, Nilu,\" he whispered into the empty verandah. \"I am breathing now.\"",
